(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-1-cns-function-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Central Nervous System Function — Physiology Exam 1 — Set 2","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"What happens to cortical function if the thalamic connection to a cortical area is lost, and which sensory pathway is the exception to passing through the thalamus?","choices":["All sensory pathways pass through the thalamus with no exceptions whatsoever in the body","Cortical function increases; the visual pathway is the sole exception, bypassing the thalamus","Cortical function is entirely unaffected; the auditory pathway is the exception, bypassing the thalamus","Cortical function stops; the olfactory tract is the exception (does not pass through the thalamus)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Each area of the cortex is connected to a specific part of the thalamus, and when that thalamic connection is lost, cortical function stops; all sensory pathways pass through the thalamus except the olfactory tract. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What percentage of the population has a left-dominant hemisphere, and what is Wernicke's area responsible for in the dominant hemisphere?","choices":["95%; Wernicke's area (up to 50% larger in the dominant hemisphere) is responsible for verbal symbolism and related intelligence","100%; Wernicke's area has no specific known function in either cerebral hemisphere at all","5%; Wernicke's area instead controls only music perception in the nondominant hemisphere","50%; Wernicke's area instead controls motor speech coordination rather than comprehension"],"answer":0,"exp":"95% of the population has a left-dominant hemisphere, where Wernicke's area (which can be up to 50% larger than in the nondominant hemisphere) is responsible for verbal symbolism and related intelligence. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What type of intelligence is the nondominant hemisphere more associated with?","choices":["Other forms of sensory intelligence, such as music and sensory feelings","Only autonomic regulation of heart rate and digestion","Verbal symbolism and language comprehension abilities","Only fine motor coordination of the hands"],"answer":0,"exp":"While the dominant hemisphere (usually left) is specialized for verbal symbolism via Wernicke's area, the nondominant hemisphere is related to other forms of sensory intelligence, such as music and sensory feelings. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What deficit results from damage to the prefrontal association area?","choices":["Complete loss of both long-term and short-term memory function entirely","Inability to keep track of simultaneous bits of information and being easily distracted, impairing concerted logical thinking","Loss of the patellar reflex and all other spinal cord stretch reflexes","Loss of vision only, with every other cognitive function fully preserved"],"answer":1,"exp":"The prefrontal association area is responsible for calling forth stored information toward a goal and concerted logical thinking; damage causes an inability to keep track of simultaneous bits of information and easy distractibility. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"In the pathway for auditory communication (hearing a word and responding), what happens after the primary auditory area recognizes a sound as a word and Wernicke's area interprets it?","choices":["The word/thought is formed, transmitted via the arcuate fasciculus to Broca's area, which activates motor programs for word formation, then signals are sent to the motor cortex to control speech muscles","Nothing further happens at all — comprehension in Wernicke's area, without any further transmission elsewhere, is considered the complete and final step in the entire pathway","The signal is transmitted directly from the primary auditory area to the cerebellum via the inferior peduncle, where it is stored permanently as a long-term motor memory trace","The signal is immediately sent from the primary auditory area to the spinal cord directly, bypassing the arcuate fasciculus, Broca's area, and the motor cortex entirely"],"answer":0,"exp":"After the primary auditory area recognizes a sound as a word and Wernicke's area interprets its meaning, the word that expresses the thought is formed, transmitted via the arcuate fasciculus to Broca's area, which activates motor programs for word formation, and finally signals are sent to the motor cortex to control the speech muscles. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is the functional consequence of destroying the visual and auditory association areas (Wernicke aphasia)?","choices":["Loss of motor speech coordination only, with comprehension fully intact","An inability to understand the written or spoken word","Complete paralysis of the muscles used for speech","Loss of all long-term declarative memory function"],"answer":1,"exp":"Wernicke aphasia results from destruction of the visual and auditory association areas and causes an inability to understand the written or spoken word — a receptive/comprehension deficit, distinct from Broca's area's motor speech deficit. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the functional division of labor between Wernicke's area and Broca's area in speech?","choices":["Both areas perform identical, fully redundant functions, each independently forming thoughts and coordinating the muscles of speech","Wernicke's area is responsible for the formation of thoughts and choice of words; Broca's area controls the motor coordination required for speech","Wernicke's area controls the motor coordination of speech muscles; Broca's area forms thoughts and chooses the words to be spoken","Neither area is actually involved in speech at all, with both functions instead localized entirely to the cerebellum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Wernicke's area is responsible for the formation of thoughts to be expressed and the choice of words, while Broca's area controls the motor coordination required for actually speaking those words. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What can result from interruption of the corpus callosum fibers connecting the two hemispheres?","choices":["Bizarre anomalies, such as the nondominant hemisphere eliciting a motor response without the dominant hemisphere (which understands spoken word) knowing why it occurred","Complete and immediate loss of vision in both eyes simultaneously and permanently, with no ability to perceive light, shape, or motion of any kind","Immediate and total loss of consciousness lasting several minutes, followed by a prolonged period of confusion and disorientation upon waking","No effect at all, since the two cerebral hemispheres function completely independently and have no need to share information via connecting fibers"],"answer":0,"exp":"The corpus callosum connects the two hemispheres and allows transfer of information; interruption of these fibers can lead to bizarre anomalies, such as the nondominant hemisphere (which understands written word) eliciting a motor response without the dominant hemisphere (which understands spoken word) knowing why the response was performed. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What are the three types of memory classified by duration?","choices":["Explicit memory (conscious recall of facts, lasting days to years) and implicit memory (unconscious skill memory, lasting a lifetime)","Sensory memory (milliseconds to seconds), working memory (seconds to minutes), and procedural memory (a lifetime)","Immediate memory (seconds to minutes), short-term memory (days to weeks), and long-term memory (years to a lifetime)","Only two types exist by duration: short-term memory (minutes to hours) and long-term memory (years to a lifetime)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Memory is classified into three types by duration: immediate memory (lasts seconds to minutes), short-term memory (lasts days to weeks), and long-term memory (lasts years or a lifetime). — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is the proposed mechanism of immediate memory?","choices":["A complete anatomical rewiring of neural circuits, in which new axon collaterals sprout to form entirely new synaptic connections","A structural increase in synapse size through synthesis of new release-site proteins, enlarging the vesicular release area","Synaptic potentiation through accumulation of calcium in the presynaptic membrane, promoting neurotransmitter release","A progressive loss of active calcium channels at the presynaptic terminal, reducing neurotransmitter release"],"answer":2,"exp":"Immediate memory may result from synaptic potentiation through the accumulation of calcium in the presynaptic membrane, which promotes neurotransmitter release; short-term memory may result from a temporary physical or chemical change in the pre- or postsynaptic membrane. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What is habituation, and what is its cellular mechanism?","choices":["A progressive decline in response sensitivity to repetitive stimulation, resulting from a progressive decline in the number of active calcium channels (less calcium entry, less transmitter released)","Sensitization -- an increased response to repetitive or noxious stimulation, resulting from facilitation of transmitter release via increased calcium influx at the presynaptic terminal","Synaptic potentiation from calcium accumulation in the presynaptic membrane, a process that strengthens rather than weakens neurotransmitter release with repeated stimulation","A structural change in synapse size due to new release-site protein synthesis, permanently increasing the area available for vesicular transmitter release"],"answer":0,"exp":"Habituation is a progressive decline in sensitivity/response resulting from repetitive stimulation, caused by a progressive decline in the number of active calcium channels at the presynaptic terminal — less calcium entry means less neurotransmitter released; it reflects the nervous system learning that a stimulus is unimportant. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What structural change underlies long-term memory formation?","choices":["A temporary chemical or physical change in the pre- or postsynaptic membrane, lasting only minutes before reversing back to baseline","A progressive decline in the number of active calcium channels at the presynaptic terminal, reducing transmitter release with each stimulation","A structural change in the synapse — an increase in the area for vesicular release (from synthesis of release site proteins), so more transmitter is released","A decrease in the area available for vesicular release, caused by degradation of release site proteins during inactivity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Long-term memory results from a structural change in the synapse: an increase in the area for vesicular release (from synthesis of release site proteins) means more transmitter is released; during periods of inactivity, this area decreases in size. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What can interrupt the consolidation of memory (converting immediate memory into short- and long-term memory)?","choices":["Deep sleep, which always interrupts consolidation since neural activity is minimized during that stage","Rehearsal, which interrupts consolidation by overloading the immediate memory circuit with competing information","Only direct physical injury to the hippocampus can interrupt it, since consolidation occurs there exclusively","Electrical shock or anesthesia, which can prevent memory development since consolidation requires time"],"answer":3,"exp":"Memory consolidation (converting immediate into short- and long-term memory, via chemical/physical/anatomical synaptic changes) requires time and can be interrupted by electrical shock or anesthesia, preventing memory development; rehearsal, by contrast, enhances consolidation. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What deficit results from hippocampal damage, and what is this condition called?","choices":["Complete loss of all spinal and brainstem reflexes, including the patellar and pupillary reflexes","Anterograde amnesia — inability to form new verbal or symbolic long-term memory","Loss of motor speech production (Broca aphasia) with intact language comprehension","Retrograde amnesia -- inability to recall memories formed before the damage occurred"],"answer":1,"exp":"The hippocampus is critical for long-term memory; damage causes anterograde amnesia — the inability to form new verbal or symbolic long-term memories — and the hippocampus also helps determine which sensory experiences are important enough to warrant attention. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What deficit results from thalamic damage related to memory, and what is this condition called?","choices":["Retrograde amnesia — the inability to recall previously stored experiences, since the thalamus scans the cortex for the stored memory's location/circuit","Only a mild deficit in sensory relay occurs, since the thalamus plays no direct role in memory storage or retrieval","A selective loss of procedural memory only, while declarative memory recall remains fully intact","Anterograde amnesia, identical to the deficit caused by hippocampal damage, since both structures scan the cortex together"],"answer":0,"exp":"Thalamic structures are important for recalling memories; damage to the thalamus causes retrograde amnesia, the inability to recall previously stored experiences, since the thalamus scans the cortex for the area and circuit holding the stored memory. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is the function of the bulboreticular facilitatory area of the brainstem?","choices":["It sends purely inhibitory signals to antigravity muscles and receives input only from the cerebellum, bypassing the thalamus entirely","It sends inhibitory signals to the reticular inhibitory area and to spinal flexor circuits, and is itself excited by input from the basal ganglia","It sends excitatory signals to antigravity muscles and to the thalamus (distributed to widespread cortical areas), and is itself excited by peripheral pain signals and cortical feedback","It exclusively governs circadian rhythm timing through direct projections to the suprachiasmatic nucleus and hypothalamic sleep centers"],"answer":2,"exp":"The bulboreticular facilitatory area sends excitatory signals to the antigravity muscles and to the thalamus (from which they distribute to widespread cortical areas); it is excited by peripheral signals (especially pain) and by positive feedback from the cortex itself. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What is the function of the reticular inhibitory area of the brainstem?","choices":["It only affects the spinal reflex arcs, suppressing flexor withdrawal reflexes without any influence on the cortex","It sends excitatory signals directly to the cortex, bypassing the bulboreticular facilitatory area and the thalamus entirely","It has no direct effect on cortical activity, acting instead solely to dampen spinal motor neuron excitability","It sends inhibitory signals to the bulboreticular facilitatory area, decreasing cortical activity when excited"],"answer":3,"exp":"The reticular inhibitory area sends inhibitory signals to the bulboreticular facilitatory area; when the inhibitory area is excited, it decreases the activity of the excitatory area and thus decreases overall cortical activity. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is the limbic system, and what functions does it govern?","choices":["A division of the peripheral autonomic nervous system responsible for regulating glandular secretion and smooth muscle tone","A single, discrete nucleus located within the cerebellar vermis, involved solely in coordinating fine motor timing","A network of interconnected brain structures beneath the cerebral cortex, around the thalamus, central to emotion, motivation, memory, and survival-related behaviors","A collection of white matter tracts exclusively involved in relaying voluntary motor commands from the cortex to the spinal cord"],"answer":2,"exp":"The limbic system is a network of interconnected brain structures beneath the cerebral cortex and around the thalamus, forming a functional hub central to generating and regulating emotional responses (fear, pleasure, anger, anxiety), memory consolidation, reward-seeking behavior, and survival instincts. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What is the amygdala's key role within the limbic system?","choices":["Fear, threat detection, and emotional learning","Permanent long-term memory storage and retrieval","Fine motor coordination of speech muscles","Autonomic regulation of body temperature"],"answer":0,"exp":"The amygdala is key for fear, threat detection, and emotional learning; it receives input from other limbic areas and most of the cortex, and sends output back to the cortex as well as into the hippocampus, septum, and hypothalamus. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which of the following is a \"vegetative\" function controlled by the hypothalamus, as the major output pathway of the limbic system?","choices":["Voluntary control of motor speech production, coordinating respiratory timing and articulation muscles together with Broca's area and the cerebellum","Neurogenic control of arterial pressure, body temperature regulation, fluid volume regulation, and endocrine gland secretion","Formation of long-term declarative memory, requiring rehearsal and consolidation over hours to days via hippocampal and thalamic circuits working together","Voluntary fine motor control of the hands, mediated through the primary motor cortex and corticospinal tract rather than any hypothalamic pathway"],"answer":1,"exp":"The hypothalamus, the major output pathway of the limbic system, controls vegetative functions including neurogenic control of arterial pressure, regulation of body temperature, regulation of fluid volume, and regulation of endocrine gland secretion (growth hormone, thyroid hormone, glucocorticoids, sex hormones). — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which hypothalamic region is associated with eating, thirst, general activity level, and rage?","choices":["Lateral hypothalamus","Periventricular nucleus","Ventromedial nucleus","Anterior hypothalamus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lateral hypothalamus is associated with eating, thirst, general level of activity, and rage; the ventromedial nucleus is associated with satiety and tranquility, and the periventricular nucleus with fear and punishment reactions. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Where is the reward center located in the brain, and which type of response (reward or punishment) takes precedence?","choices":["Reward center around the medial forebrain bundle (lateral and ventromedial hypothalamus); punishment always takes precedence over reward","Reward center around the septal nuclei and anterior cingulate cortex; reward always takes precedence over punishment","Reward and punishment centers overlap entirely within the amygdala, so neither system takes functional precedence","Punishment center in the central gray around the aqueduct extending into periventricular zones; reward always takes precedence over punishment"],"answer":0,"exp":"The reward center is located around the medial forebrain bundle (lateral and ventromedial hypothalamus), while the punishment center is located in the central gray around the aqueduct extending into periventricular zones; punishment always takes precedence over reward. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What is the evolutionary origin of the hippocampus, and how does this relate to its function?","choices":["It originated as part of the olfactory cortex, historically involved in determining the importance of incoming information (e.g., is it food, danger, or a mate) via smell","It originated as part of the visual cortex; in lower animals, vision was the dominant sense for detecting predators, and the early hippocampus helped discriminate threatening shapes and movement","It has no evolutionary connection to sensory processing at all, instead arising as a late elaboration of the neocortex devoted to abstract symbolic reasoning","It originated as part of the primitive motor cortex; in lower animals, stereotyped motor sequences were critical for survival, and the early hippocampus helped select which movement pattern to execute"],"answer":0,"exp":"The hippocampus originated as part of the olfactory cortex; in lower animals, smell was an important determinant of behavior (is it good to eat, does it smell dangerous, is it sexually inviting), and the early hippocampus was involved in determining the importance of incoming sensory information. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"How do reward and punishment influence whether the hippocampus stores information as memory?","choices":["Only punishment-associated experiences are consolidated into memory; pleasurable experiences are processed instead by the amygdala and are never stored as long-term memory by the hippocampus","Reward and punishment have no bearing on hippocampal storage -- the hippocampus stores every sensory experience equally regardless of its emotional significance","A person becomes habituated to indifferent stimuli, but learns (stores as memory) sensory experiences that cause pain or pleasure — the hippocampus drives rehearsal and consolidation of these experiences","Only reward-associated experiences drive hippocampal consolidation; painful experiences are instead routed exclusively through the spinal withdrawal reflex circuitry"],"answer":2,"exp":"A person becomes habituated to indifferent stimuli but learns any sensory experience that causes pain or pleasure; the hippocampus provides the drive to rehearse and consolidate these emotionally significant sensory experiences into memory. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"What is the functional role of the cingulate gyrus within the limbic system?","choices":["Exclusively regulating pituitary hormone secretion","Integrating emotion with cognition and attention","Detecting angular acceleration of head rotation","Generating the motor pattern for speech sounds"],"answer":1,"exp":"The cingulate gyrus integrates emotion with cognition and attention, serving as a key limbic structure linking emotional processing to higher cognitive function. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What is the functional significance of the suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus?","choices":["It serves as the primary site of long-term declarative memory storage","It functions as the body's \"master clock,\" regulating circadian rhythms","It controls only the consensual pupillary light reflex","It functions solely to relay retinal input to the visual cortex"],"answer":1,"exp":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus functions as the master clock of the body, coordinating circadian rhythms. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Does destruction of the cerebral cortex prevent an individual from thinking, according to this lecture?","choices":["Thinking occurs entirely within the cerebellum, since the cortex functions solely as a relay station for raw sensory data and has no role in generating conscious thought","Yes -- thinking is impossible without an intact cortex, since the cortex is the sole structure capable of generating any pattern of neural activity","The neural mechanism of thought is fully understood and is localized entirely to the prefrontal cortex, with no other brain region contributing","No — destruction of the cerebral cortex does not prevent thinking, though depth of thought and level of awareness may be reduced"],"answer":3,"exp":"Destruction of the cerebral cortex does not prevent an individual from thinking, although the depth of thought and level of awareness may be reduced; the neural mechanism underlying thought itself is not fully known, but is thought to involve a specific pattern of simultaneous neural activity across many brain areas. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What is a \"memory trace\"?","choices":["A type of neurotransmitter released specifically during rehearsal that binds calcium channels to strengthen synapses","A physical scar of degenerated tissue left behind permanently at the site of a formerly active synapse","A specialized structure found only within Purkinje cells of the cerebellum that stores motor sequences","A specific pattern or pathway of signal transmission that, once established, can be reactivated by the thinking mind to reproduce the memory"],"answer":3,"exp":"A memory trace is a specific pattern or pathway of signal transmission resulting from a change in synaptic transmission capability; once established, it can be activated by the thinking mind to reproduce the associated memory. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"In the pathway for visual communication (reading a word aloud), what happens after visual input is processed in the parietal-temporal-occipital association cortex (angular gyrus)?","choices":["The signal is discarded at the angular gyrus, since visual word input cannot be converted to a phonetic representation without first passing through the primary auditory cortex","It is relayed to the hippocampus for permanent storage as a visual memory trace, without ever being converted into a motor speech pattern or reaching Broca's area","It bypasses Wernicke's area entirely, passing straight from the angular gyrus to Broca's area and then to the motor cortex for speech muscles","Visual input reaches full interpretation in Wernicke's area, then passes to Broca's area for motor formation of the word before signals go to the motor cortex for speech muscles"],"answer":3,"exp":"For visual communication, after the primary visual area receives input and it is processed in the angular gyrus region, the visual input reaches full interpretation in Wernicke's area, then passes to Broca's area for motor formation of the word, and finally to the motor cortex to control speech muscles. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What effect does stimulation of a \"facilitator terminal\" have on habituation?","choices":["It converts habituation into long-term memory instantly","It has no effect on habituation","It prevents habituation from occurring","It accelerates habituation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Stimulation of a facilitator terminal prevents habituation, counteracting the progressive decline in calcium channel activity and neurotransmitter release that normally underlies habituation. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 46"}]};
